<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Will making my home quieter make my tinnitus louder?</h3>
<p>Total silence can make tinnitus feel louder for many people. Aim for gentle sound enrichment—like a soft fan or low, steady nature sounds—rather than silence. Keep it at a comfortable, not mask-all, level. If you’re unsure what’s right for you, an audiologist can tailor a sound plan that supports both sleep and daytime comfort.</p>

<h3>What indoor noise levels should I aim for?</h3>
<p>Think in ranges and trends, not perfection. Many public health guidelines point toward keeping typical indoor living spaces in roughly the mid-30s to mid-40s dBA and bedrooms quieter at night, around the low-30s dBA when feasible. Your exact targets depend on your building, street noise, and comfort. Each few dB you shave off improves clarity and reduces effort.</p>

<h3>Will acoustic panels help if I already wear hearing aids?</h3>
<p>Yes. Hearing aids amplify what’s in the room—including echo. Panels and other soft surfaces lower reverberation, so amplified speech is cleaner and easier to understand. Even a few well-placed panels, a rug with a thick pad, and curtains can noticeably boost speech clarity and reduce listening fatigue.</p>

<h3>Do plants really help with sound?</h3>
<p>Plants aren’t magic absorbers, but tall, leafy groups can scatter sound and take a slight edge off echo, especially in combination with rugs, curtains, and bookshelves. Think of them as part of a layered approach, not a standalone fix.</p>


